.TH smi-sendmail "1" "August 2026" "SMI Toolset" "SMI Server Command"
.SH NAME
smi-sendmail \- Send an e-mail message via SMTP using curl
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B smi-sendmail
.I RECIPIENT
.I SUBJECT
.RI [ MESSAGE
.RI [ HOST [: PORT ]]]
.br
.B echo
.I MESSAGE
.B |
.B smi-sendmail
.I RECIPIENT
.I SUBJECT
.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
.B smi-sendmail
sends a plain-text e-mail message via SMTP using
.BR curl (1).
No switches are required; all connection parameters default to the
local GreenMail test server started by
.BR smi-greenmail (1).
.PP
Arguments are positional: recipient first, subject second, message body third.
If the message body argument is omitted, the body is read from standard input,
allowing pipeline use.
.PP
SMTP connection details and credentials are taken from environment variables
so that they can be adjusted without changing the command line.
The sender address defaults to
.IB username @smtp.test
where
.I username
is the current OS login name.
.SH ARGUMENTS
.TP
.I RECIPIENT
Destination e-mail address (e.g.\&
.BR user@smtp.test ).
.TP
.I SUBJECT
Message subject line.
Quote the argument if it contains spaces.
.TP
.I MESSAGE
Message body text.
Optional; if absent, body is read from standard input.
.TP
.IB HOST [: PORT ]
SMTP server to use for this invocation only.
Overrides
.SM SMI_SMTP_HOST
and
.SM SMI_SMTP_PORT
for this call without changing the environment.
.I PORT
is optional; if omitted only the host is overridden and the current port
(environment or default) is kept.
Example:
.BR mail.example.com:587 .
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B SMI_SMTP_HOST
SMTP server hostname or IP address.
Default:
.BR 127.0.0.1 .
.TP
.B SMI_SMTP_PORT
SMTP server port.
Default:
.BR 3025 .
.TP
.B SMI_SMTP_USER
SMTP authentication username.
Default: current OS login name.
.TP
.B SMI_SMTP_PASSWORD
SMTP authentication password.
Default: login name with
.B 123
appended.
.TP
.B SMI_SMTP_FROM
Sender address used in the
.B MAIL FROM
envelope and the
.B From:
header.
Default:
.IB username @smtp.test\fR.
.SH EXAMPLES
.PP
Send with all defaults (body as argument):
.RS
.B smi-sendmail has@smtp.test "Hello" "Body text here"
.RE
.PP
Send with body from a pipeline:
.RS
.B echo "Build failed on main" | smi-sendmail ops@smtp.test "CI alert"
.RE
.PP
Send to a remote SMTP server via the 4th argument:
.RS
.B smi-sendmail bob@example.com "Hi Bob" "Hello from the CLI" mail.example.com:587
.RE
.PP
Override host only, keep default port:
.RS
.B smi-sendmail bob@example.com "Hi Bob" "Hello" mail.example.com
.RE
.PP
Override via environment instead:
.RS
.nf
SMI_SMTP_HOST=mail.example.com SMI_SMTP_PORT=587 \\
SMI_SMTP_USER=alice SMI_SMTP_PASSWORD=secret \\
SMI_SMTP_FROM=alice@example.com \\
smi-sendmail bob@example.com "Hi Bob" "Hello from the CLI"
.fi
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR smi-greenmail (1),
.BR curl (1)
